> I think this example is broken since {lo vrici gugde} or {lo gugde poi 
> vrici} can describe even one country various in vrici2.
>
> I suppose that somehow we need to show that there were different from 
> each other.
> Is it just {noi simxu lo ka ce'u ce'u nalsimsa} or can we use {ni'e 
> vrici} here?
>
I think {lo vrici gugde} is fine:

{ lo (so'o) vrici gugde } ~> { lo poi'i ke'a gugde (gi'e so'o mei) gi'e 
vrici (lo ka du) } ~> { lo (so'o) gugde poi vrici (lo ka du) }

I think that including {so'o} would be unnecessary, because I think {lo 
pa mei cu vrici} doesn't make much sense.

